      en: "The Lower Mesopotamian Plain extends over southern Iraq and covers approximately 150,000 km². It is traversed by the Euphrates and Tigris rivers, which join in the Shatt al-Arab. This flat alluvial plain is extremely fertile and forms the heartland of ancient irrigation agriculture. The average elevation is less than 100 meters above sea level. Traditionally, rice, wheat, and dates are cultivated here. However, the region is increasingly affected by salinization and desertification. Numerous archaeological sites such as Ur and Babylon lie on this plain. It is also an important wetland system for migratory birds.",
