      en: "Mount Sork is a mountain in the Zagros range of Iraqi Kurdistan, notable for its Neo-Assyrian rock reliefs dating to the reign of Sargon II (722–705 BCE). The reliefs depict the king performing religious ceremonies and include cuneiform inscriptions that provide insights into Assyrian royal ideology. The site was first brought to Western attention by Austen Henry Layard in the 1840s, but systematic archaeological work began only in the 1990s. The mountain rises to approximately 2,100 meters and is characterized by steep limestone slopes. The surrounding area is sparsely populated, with traditional pastoralism being the main economic activity.",
